let the arguments commence so um this is  something I've been meaning to talk  about and an animation junkie for a long  time and it kept sort of slipping off my  radar it came back now because now I've  got the C space cowboy podcast going  where I'm revisiting Cowboy Bebop and  one of the things that I have to account  for when I talk about my reviews of  those episodes is that I'm watching the  dubbed episodes I'm not watching the  subtitled episodes um and I didn't go  into a ton of detail as to why I make  that call but I kind of want to do that  here  because I put a lot of thought into the  whole argument of dubbed versus  subtitled and I watched dubbed anime I'm  not ashamed of that I have no shame in  that and more to the point I think  that's the better way to watch it and  I'm going to explain why now first thing  I'm gonna say is everything that I am  about to say about subtitles vs dubs is  specific to anime and animation in  general I do not believe that you should  watch live-action movies dubbed not for  the obvious reason which is that it's  way more obvious that the mouths aren't  syncing up that's actually not that much  of a problem in anime because the mouths  usually just have generic movements  anyways and they don't even perfectly  sync up with the original Japanese a lot  of the time so that's not so much an  issue there but the big thing with why I  will not watch a dubbed live-action  movie is I cannot expect that an actor  in a booth reading a translated script  is going to be able to evoke the same  motion that an actor on set in that  moment working in that environment was  able to get and I say that even  understanding and knowing enough about  movies to know you know 80  a lot of times and actors depending on  where the thing is set especially any  exterior scenes often have to read of  their own lines but if it's the actor  who did it originally they can pull from  their own experience and they're  remembering of being there they have the  ability to recreate that I just don't  think you can get the same performance  out of a guy in the out of a guy in a  booth that somebody who was on set could  it possibly given however what we're  talking about animation the original was  also just the guy in a booth which means  that at least that big concern for me  doesn't exist in anime so allow me to  talk a little bit about the pluses and  minuses of dubbing and subbing with  anime my eye out for dubs pretty much  every time I actually don't see a lot of  advantage in subtitles unless you are  somebody who is trying to learn Japanese  and you want to hear it more and you  just need the subs like as a guide for  wait what was that oh that's right like  okay sure  that circumstance maybe but other than  that you know it's the weirdest argument  for me is is people who go well I want  to hear it in its original language I  want to hear it as the filmmaker  intended sounds good but I'm also pretty  sure the filmmaker didn't intend you to  be staring at the bottom two inches of  the screen and I think that detracts a  heck of a lot more from what you are  supposed to be focusing on than the fact  that the language is different than it  was originally like that's an argument I  will never get I get hung up with the  same thing with with manga when people  like say oh the only way to read manga  are the ones that are printed right to  left as in the original Japanese except  they're also reading one that's been  translated so again unless you're  reading in the original Japanese why  would you do that because you're the  in Japanese that it's that they read  right to left is because that's the  direction they read their language as  well so for you as an American to read  the book right to left but still read  the captions left to right it's  completely disorienting it's not the  original intention of it you're just  there just being a snob at that point  you're trying to act like you're being  more authentic when you're just making  the experience weird and awkward but  that's just a minor tangent of manga off  of that point that just bugs me but  beyond that ultimately the big thing for  me the only reason that I will ever go  with subtitles in an anime is if the dub  sucks  and sometimes the dub sucks one of my  favorite anime movies I love the ghost  in the shell' it's it's not my number  one anymore but it was for a long time I  love that movie to death and I cannot  watch it dubbed the dub is terrible it's  just a flat unengaging boring voiceover  it's just bad and there's a couple of  others escaflowne I tend to favor the  subtitles because I don't like the dub  on a lot of the characters but the thing  is escaflowne ghost in the shell' and  certainly things that predate them  things being brought over to a metaphor  American audiences from Japan were it  was treated as a niche product it was  treated very much as a veil like  whatever we give them just get some  voices on and get it out there there  wasn't a heck of a lot of care put into  the voice acting of anime through the  80s and much of the 90s once we started  to get into the 2000s actually around  the time Cowboy Bebop started to make it  over here we started to get more care  put in  to it and there were people working on  these dubs who were working harder to  have decent performances than this and  at this point like I can't remember the  last time that I saw a an anime film  with a lousy dub I honestly don't  remember what the last one was now I  mean again bear in mind while I enjoy  anime I'm not like deep it's not I'm I  always hesitate to call myself an anime  fan not because I'm not a fan of anime I  am but generally when people label  themselves anime fans they like can  start rattling off you know all these  characters and all these shows that I'd  never seen so you know it's just the  depth of expectation when someone calls  themselves an anime fan but I watch a  decent amount of it I do not remember  the last time I saw it flat out bad dub  it's been years it's been decades it's  so I don't see the value in watching an  anime with subtitles it's distracting  and in a way that I think is way more  distracting than anything that dub could  be again unless the dub is absolute crap  because bringing it back to ghost in the  shell' I love that movie pretty much  immediately but I also had to watch it  twice basically back-to-back to get the  proper experience I had to watch it once  reading it because the there was there's  some anime where the dialogue doesn't  matter as much or like there's little  snippets and then there's a lot of  action goes to the shells of really  heady movie what they talk about is  important and it matters to the plots of  the themes to the characters so you need  to be paying attention and so I had to  watch twice once reading it and then a  second time have now basically knowing  it just glancing down like oh right  that's what they're talking about and  then properly watching and you know you  can make an argument all well that  forces you to have a deeper experience I  shouldn't be forced to see something  twice to get the  parents that I can get out of watching  most other things once if I watch  something a second time I wanted to be  because I want to see it a second time  not because I need to because I feel  like I didn't get the proper experience  yet so for those reasons I will always  always favor dubs now I don't mean to  come across as a jerk and I think I'm I  may have risked doing so because I'm  sure there are people out there who  prefer subtitles but if your reason for  preferring subtitles is something other  than I have what I've mentioned please  let me know because I don't I don't  understand I don't understand what you  gain from reading subtitles as opposed  to getting able to actually drink in the  visuals and just hear the words as  opposed to having to work overtime to  try and follow what's going on s